.TH CVECTOR_SORT_WITH_COMPARISON_PROC 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc \- sort vector with custom comparison
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc(v, comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sorts the vector in place through the csort library with a custom
comparison function, which follows the standard comparator convention of
returning a value <0, 0 or >0.

This is the supported way to sort a vector whose element type has no
default comparison procedure, that is the element types for which
cvec_sort refuses to compile: a struct, a union, a pointer to anything
other than char, and a fixed-size char array. It sorts a type that does
have a default comparison procedure as well, and then the procedure you
pass defines the order.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v                 Vector to sort. The macro evaluates it exactly once.
comparison_proc   Comparison function for sorting. The macro evaluates it
                  exactly once.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The sort is the stable, in-place mergesort of csort, which moves the
elements inside the backing array directly with no call per element
access. It takes O(n log n) time and O(n) space. Asserts if v is NULL.

A NULL comparison_proc makes it call _ccol_fatal_err(). The comparison
procedure comes from the caller's own expression, whose value in general
isn't known before the program runs, so this is a run-time check. The
element-type check of cvec_sort is a compile-time _Static_assert
instead, and a cvec_sort call never gets to this diagnostic.

If the internal temporary buffer of the sort can't be allocated, the
macro calls _ccol_fatal_err(), as every other type-inferred macro in
cvector.h that changes a vector does (cvec_push, cvec_reserve,
cvec_append_array and the others). The vector is then left completely
unsorted.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
int compare_ints(const void *a, const void *b) {
  int x = *(const int *)a, y = *(const int *)b;
  return (x > y) - (x < y);
}
cvec_construct(vec, int);
// ... add elements ...
cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc(vec, compare_ints);
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_sort (3),
.BR csort_sort (3)
